  "summary": "A decorative technique where layers of colored plaster are scratched through to reveal contrasting colors beneath.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate patterns and designs created by scratching through upper layers of plaster to expose different colored layers beneath. This technique was widely used in Renaissance Italy and later in various European traditions for architectural decoration, particularly on building facades and interior walls.",
  "culturalContext": "Sgraffito on walls has been used in Europe since classical times. It was popularized in Italy in the 15th and 16th centuries and can be found in African art. In combination with ornamental decoration, these techniques formed an alternative to the prevailing painting of walls. The technical procedure for sgraffito is relatively simple, resembling the process of painting frescoes.",
